thub.io/post/2024-05-01-algebraic.dist/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algebra</media:title></media:content><category>statistics</category><category>probability</category><category>distributions</category><category>R</category><category>functional programming</category><category>algebra</category><category>sicp</category></item><item><title>Polynomials as Euclidean Domains</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-07-polynomials-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Tue, 14 Jul 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-07-polynomials-stepanov/</guid><description>The same GCD algorithm works for integers and polynomials because both are Euclidean domains. One structure, many types, same algorithms.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-07-polynomials-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algebra</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algebra</category><category>polynomials</category><category>euclidean-domain</category></item><item><title>Modular Arithmetic as Rings</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-06-modular-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Sat, 22 Jun 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-06-modular-stepanov/</guid><description>Integers modulo N form a ring, an algebraic structure that determines which algorithms apply. Understanding this structure unlocks algorithms from cryptography to competitive programming.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-06-modular-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Algebra</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>number-theory</category><category>rings</category><category>algebra</category></item></channel></r